00:05:15.768 --> 00:05:17.578
And then I'm going to show you some pictures.

00:05:18.728 --> 00:05:23.218
The banana was basically unknown in the United States and in our hemisphere until about

00:05:23.228 --> 00:05:27.538
1875, when it was imported for the 1876

00:05:27.548 --> 00:05:31.378
Centennial Exhibition. And remember this when I'm showing you the slides.

00:05:31.388 --> 00:05:34.038
Bananas were so unknown that...

00:05:34.048 --> 00:05:36.418
People didn't really understand how to eat them.

00:05:36.688 --> 00:05:39.798
In addition, bananas were considered taboo because of their shape.

00:05:39.908 --> 00:05:43.618
And so people who did eat bananas would sort of have to cut them into

00:05:43.628 --> 00:05:46.618
non -suggestive, let's say,

00:05:47.208 --> 00:05:49.118
forms that didn't make them taste very good.

00:05:49.668 --> 00:05:54.358
When a couple of entrepreneurs decided to bring some bananas north, mostly on a lark,

00:05:54.368 --> 00:05:59.338
they owed some money and they thought they could sell some bananas, they lit a fire.

00:06:00.218 --> 00:06:04.488
That sort of began the fresh fruit market in the United

00:06:04.498 --> 00:06:05.268
States.

00:06:05.848 --> 00:06:09.918
But they had to teach people how to use bananas, how to eat bananas.

00:06:09.928 --> 00:06:12.758
Nobody knew what a banana was.

00:06:13.188 --> 00:06:16.158
And in addition, they had a marketing strategy.

00:06:16.188 --> 00:06:20.398
The marketing strategy was to make bananas the cheapest fruit in the supermarket.

00:06:20.408 --> 00:06:22.678
That was how they were going to beat apples and oranges.

00:06:22.828 --> 00:06:24.418
Well, how would they do that?

00:06:24.648 --> 00:06:28.818
They would do it by taking over countries, by controlling the cost of labor,

00:06:29.048 --> 00:06:30.858
controlling the cost of marketing.

00:06:31.048 --> 00:06:35.218
And so the banana became the cheapest fruit on the backs and lives of

00:06:35.228 --> 00:06:37.278
thousands and millions of people.

00:06:37.288 --> 00:06:38.758
A lot of bloodshed.

00:06:38.768 --> 00:06:40.478
I'm really moving fast now.

00:06:40.648 --> 00:06:45.338
Almost immediately after the banana was grown, the banana was a monoculture.

00:06:45.348 --> 00:06:48.718
There were over a thousand banana varieties, but we really only get one.

00:06:49.368 --> 00:06:52.298
And that one is...

00:06:52.308 --> 00:06:54.758
That one is, there's a reason there's only one.

00:06:54.768 --> 00:06:57.758
There's a, same way there's a reason McDonald's only sells hamburgers.

00:06:57.768 --> 00:07:00.658
It's cheaper to have a limited stock.

00:07:01.238 --> 00:07:05.988
And so by relying on the monoculture, the banana companies introduced a disease to their

00:07:05.998 --> 00:07:08.828
first iteration, banana 1.0, let's say.

00:07:08.898 --> 00:07:11.228
That was a banana called the Gros Michel banana.

00:07:11.238 --> 00:07:13.068
It was a banana that tasted better.

00:07:13.078 --> 00:07:15.888
It was better in every way than the banana we eat now.

00:07:16.038 --> 00:07:18.308
But it was susceptible to these diseases.

00:07:19.138 --> 00:07:22.448
And over time, the banana companies...

00:07:22.488 --> 00:07:26.878
They poison the land where they wanted to grow the fruit, had to move on.

00:07:26.888 --> 00:07:29.138
They stole more land. They killed more people.

00:07:29.168 --> 00:07:33.858
And by the 1950s, this first banana, the delicious Gros Michel, was functionally

00:07:33.868 --> 00:07:38.318
commercially extinct. It does exist, but it's no longer a commercially viable product.

00:07:38.328 --> 00:07:41.158
And it was replaced with our banana, the Cavendish.

00:07:41.448 --> 00:07:44.618
The Cavendish is not a very good -tasting banana.

00:07:44.648 --> 00:07:48.458
I've tasted over 300 varieties, including several dozen in

00:07:48.508 --> 00:07:52.358
India where... India is the number one banana growing country.

00:07:52.868 --> 00:07:56.958
They have delicious, amazing bananas there that many of you will never get to taste,

00:07:56.968 --> 00:07:58.018
although I hope you do.

00:07:59.408 --> 00:08:03.498
In India, the Cavendish is called the hotel banana because it's only served to tourists

00:08:03.508 --> 00:08:05.158
and hotels who don't know better.

00:08:05.528 --> 00:08:09.438
The Cavendish took over the banana market, but starting in the

00:08:09.448 --> 00:08:13.258
1990s, late 1980s, 1990s, this disease returned.

00:08:13.328 --> 00:08:15.718
A different version of it that affected the Cavendish.

00:08:15.728 --> 00:08:18.478
And in my book, It was at the very beginning of that.

00:08:18.488 --> 00:08:21.838
It was around 2005 that the disease began to move.

00:08:22.768 --> 00:08:27.098
And although I didn't make a prediction in the book, we sort of said this banana

00:08:27.108 --> 00:08:31.398
apocalypse is coming and if the banana companies don't do anything about it, we're going

00:08:31.408 --> 00:08:34.158
to lose this banana just like we lost the last one.

00:08:34.168 --> 00:08:37.498
And the banana companies sort of denied it, continued to deny it.

00:08:37.608 --> 00:08:42.098
Well, over the past 20 years, that disease has traveled oceans, traveled continents.

00:08:42.168 --> 00:08:46.678
When I wrote the book, it was only in Asia and possibly in Australia.

00:08:46.728 --> 00:08:51.038
Now we know it's in Asia, Australia, the Philippines,

00:08:51.468 --> 00:08:52.158
Africa,

00:08:52.908 --> 00:08:54.778
Central America and South America.

00:08:54.788 --> 00:08:57.158
In other words, it's everywhere bananas are grown.

00:08:57.368 --> 00:08:59.875
Has it affected the global banana supply yet?

00:09:00.368 --> 00:09:03.118
No, because there's still a lot of places to grow bananas.

00:09:03.148 --> 00:09:07.838
But I can tell you for sure that the banana companies are working desperately to

00:09:07.848 --> 00:09:11.438
find a solution, to figure out what the replacement banana is.

00:09:11.958 --> 00:09:15.608
What I'd urge all of you guys to do, and again, I made this story really, really quick,

00:09:15.708 --> 00:09:18.718
is to think about monoculture.

00:09:18.728 --> 00:09:23.058
Think about how damaging it is to the environment, to the millions of people who rely on

00:09:23.068 --> 00:09:26.438
bananas for their food, for their main source of calories.

00:09:28.868 --> 00:09:32.918
Remember that monoculture, that a single variety of banana is not just cheating your

00:09:32.928 --> 00:09:35.198
taste buds. It's cheating nature.

00:09:35.208 --> 00:09:39.958
It's cheating science. It's cheating millions of people who rely on bananas for food and

00:09:39.968 --> 00:09:41.208
economic income.

00:09:41.395 --> 00:09:45.565
Okay, so in any case, what I was saying is that,

00:09:45.575 --> 00:09:50.215
you know, as we look forward to a new variety of

00:09:50.225 --> 00:09:54.655
banana and the banana companies are desperately working on them, I would urge people to

00:09:54.665 --> 00:09:59.155
think not just about one banana, but how magical it would be to have 10 different

00:09:59.165 --> 00:10:00.615
bananas in your supermarket.

00:10:00.885 --> 00:10:03.255
When I was a kid, we had two different kinds of apples.

00:10:03.265 --> 00:10:05.735
We called them red and green, you know, and I'm not that old.

00:10:05.995 --> 00:10:08.945
Now we have dozens of kinds of apples and they're amazing.

00:10:08.955 --> 00:10:12.305
We have many kinds of citrus that we never saw 20 years ago.

00:10:12.315 --> 00:10:17.045
And yet the banana companies, even today, continue to believe that a single banana is the

00:10:17.055 --> 00:10:20.905
solution to the problem that a single banana caused.

00:10:20.935 --> 00:10:25.665
And I can promise you that the world, the people who

00:10:25.675 --> 00:10:29.805
rely on bananas for their calories, people who rely on for their incomes.

00:10:30.825 --> 00:10:35.375
People who eat bananas in every hemisphere, every north, south, east, west,

00:10:35.385 --> 00:10:38.335
will be happier if we get these great bananas.

00:10:38.345 --> 00:10:42.125
And I'm going to tell you a little bit about some of these as I do this presentation.

00:10:42.250 --> 00:10:44.640
So the banana, the atheist nightmare.

00:10:44.650 --> 00:10:48.980
What does this mean? Look at all these things that prove that the banana was

00:10:48.990 --> 00:10:53.060
created by a higher power than humanity.

00:10:53.090 --> 00:10:55.400
Shaped for the human hand, non -slip surface.

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:58.440
I don't know anyone who thinks banana peels aren't slippery, but...

00:10:58.450 --> 00:11:03.160
We'll leave that alone. Outward indicators of contents, pad for removal of wrapper,

00:11:03.170 --> 00:11:07.760
perforated, biodegradable, shape for the mouth, pleasing to the taste buds, curved

00:11:07.770 --> 00:11:12.480
towards the face. All these things are true, yet what it proves actually is

00:11:12.490 --> 00:11:13.260
the opposite.

00:11:13.890 --> 00:11:18.340
Whether or not you believe in a higher power, the banana was created by man.

00:11:32.224 --> 00:11:36.774
That curve is because the curved bananas were easier to eat and so we grew them

00:11:36.784 --> 00:11:41.014
more. The ripening characteristics, and not all bananas have these ripening

00:11:41.024 --> 00:11:45.734
characteristics, are because we have chosen a specific breed of banana and hybridized

00:11:45.744 --> 00:11:48.694
it to make it have those indicators.

00:11:48.704 --> 00:11:53.334
Every one of these things is actually proof that the banana is the product of

00:11:53.344 --> 00:11:57.974
humanity. And this is really important because it really is the first agricultural

00:11:57.984 --> 00:12:00.214
product to be seen this way.

00:12:00.224 --> 00:12:02.294
10,000, 15,000 years ago.

00:12:02.304 --> 00:12:05.254
The banana was, you know, a wild thing.

00:12:05.264 --> 00:12:07.574
We'll look at some wild bananas in a second.

00:12:07.584 --> 00:12:10.054
And it evolved for people and with people.

00:12:10.064 --> 00:12:13.574
The banana has followed us and we have taken it around the world.

00:12:13.584 --> 00:12:18.214
And there's a very strong argument to be made that if the banana hadn't existed, if this

00:12:18.224 --> 00:12:21.214
easy to grow, easy to transport.

00:12:22.272 --> 00:12:26.822
high calorie food had not traveled with us if we had not learned how to grow it we might

00:12:26.832 --> 00:12:30.422
never have expanded around the world the way we did and one thing that's really

00:12:30.432 --> 00:12:34.822
interesting is if you follow the name for banana in many many native

00:12:34.832 --> 00:12:39.542
languages whether those languages are in africa whether they're in samoa whether

00:12:39.552 --> 00:12:41.412
they're in papua new guinea

00:12:42.032 --> 00:12:47.012
whether they're anywhere in on the continent of asia a lot of those words are the same

00:12:47.072 --> 00:12:51.982
because the banana traveled with us sometimes over thousands of miles sometimes by sea

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:55.670
And so the banana is not the atheist nightmare.

00:12:55.680 --> 00:12:58.980
It's actually the atheist dream come true.

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:00.982
We'll move on here.

00:13:01.696 --> 00:13:05.396
These are some fanciful banana images, neither of which are real.

00:13:05.696 --> 00:13:10.166
The one on the left is a sort of classic 1800s botanical painting.

00:13:10.176 --> 00:13:13.526
And you can see at the top the bananas growing, and bananas do grow like that.

00:13:13.536 --> 00:13:16.686
You might notice that they appear to grow upside down.

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:21.990
And then at the bottom is the male part of the plant, which in non-sterile

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:24.950
bananas is the fertilizing part.

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:29.670
And on the right then is another non-sterile banana creation, the Chiquita

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:31.030
Banana Lady.

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:32.750
This is a very modern

00:13:33.744 --> 00:13:35.364
incarnation of her.

00:13:35.824 --> 00:13:40.564
She talks about nutrition of bananas, but back in the early days when she was first

00:13:41.344 --> 00:13:46.054
introduced, her job was to teach people how to eat bananas because we didn't know.

00:13:46.064 --> 00:13:50.534
And so the Chiquita song said, if a banana is flecked with brown and has a golden hue,

00:13:50.544 --> 00:13:52.694
bananas are the best and are the best for you.

00:13:52.704 --> 00:13:54.294
So there was a bit of education in there.

00:13:54.304 --> 00:13:57.094
People didn't know a banana needed to be flecked with brown.

00:13:57.104 --> 00:13:59.764
They didn't know whether it could be eaten green or yellow.

00:13:59.984 --> 00:14:03.814
And so The reason these two pictures are together is to show that the banana is not just

00:14:03.824 --> 00:14:08.374
a sort of fanciful idea, but it is also even now the product of

00:14:08.384 --> 00:14:12.374
human ingenuity, human marketing, and sometimes human brutality.

00:14:12.384 --> 00:14:14.102
And we'll talk about that in a second.

00:14:15.872 --> 00:14:17.782
These ladies holding the bananas.

00:14:17.792 --> 00:14:18.472
This is

00:14:18.992 --> 00:14:22.662
a card issued by the United Fruit Company, which is now

00:14:22.672 --> 00:14:26.692
Chiquita, around 1885.

00:14:26.832 --> 00:14:31.142
And you can see these are very proper Victorian ladies and they're all holding bananas.

00:14:31.152 --> 00:14:32.342
Why are they doing this?

00:14:32.352 --> 00:14:34.070
Why was this promotional?

00:14:34.208 --> 00:14:38.198
Well, it's because bananas were a taboo because of their suggestive

00:14:38.208 --> 00:14:42.998
shape. And in order to get the moms and housewives and ladies of

00:14:43.008 --> 00:14:47.638
19th century America to adopt this fruit, the banana marketers, who were absolute

00:14:47.648 --> 00:14:51.958
geniuses, introduced tens of thousands of these

00:14:51.968 --> 00:14:55.438
postcards showing the kinds of ladies you might see on

00:14:56.480 --> 00:14:58.660
You know, the golden age or something,

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:04.260
holding bananas and making them acceptable for the proper family.

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:06.230
To the right, advice about bananas.

00:15:06.240 --> 00:15:10.230
And you can see there, this is around the same time, and there are actual

00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:13.974
instructions on what to do with bananas because people didn't know.

00:15:16.688 --> 00:15:19.238
And there was all kinds of other promotional materials.

00:15:19.248 --> 00:15:23.158
And if you think about this, the idea that there would be promotional material for a

00:15:23.168 --> 00:15:25.878
fruit, a product of nature, seemed absurd.

00:15:25.888 --> 00:15:28.278
I mean, there was promotional materials for patent medicines.

00:15:28.288 --> 00:15:31.718
There was promotional materials for alcohol products.

00:15:31.728 --> 00:15:34.278
There's promotional materials for candy.

00:15:34.288 --> 00:15:38.678
But this idea that children's literature would be commissioned about bananas by

00:15:38.688 --> 00:15:43.238
United Fruit, Navichaquita, or that they would recommend, on the other side,

00:15:43.248 --> 00:15:45.348
recommended by scientists.

00:15:45.568 --> 00:15:49.078
Fruit Dispatch Company was another name for what's Navichaquita.

00:15:49.088 --> 00:15:51.558
And so there was education, there was promotion.

00:15:51.568 --> 00:15:53.238
And this had never happened.

00:15:53.248 --> 00:15:55.278
And the idea that.

00:15:55.552 --> 00:16:00.372
This would happen with a fruit was really kind of crazy, but it did work.

00:16:00.912 --> 00:16:04.742
In 1900, when bananas really started to arrive in the US in

00:16:04.752 --> 00:16:08.382
quantity, the goal was to make bananas the

00:16:08.896 --> 00:16:10.726
Biggest selling fruit in the country.

00:16:10.736 --> 00:16:13.846
Very difficult to do because bananas are expensive to produce.

00:16:13.856 --> 00:16:15.606
They're tropical. They go bad quickly.

00:16:15.616 --> 00:16:17.606
They require a lot of transportation.

00:16:17.616 --> 00:16:20.000
Apples and oranges were the most expensive fruit.

00:16:20.040 --> 00:16:23.790
Apples because they were used for eating in hard cider, oranges because they were grown

00:16:23.800 --> 00:16:25.630
in Florida and California.

00:16:25.640 --> 00:16:29.950
But because of this marketing and because of some political practices that were not very

00:16:29.960 --> 00:16:34.430
great, and we'll talk about those, the banana became the number one fruit

00:16:34.440 --> 00:16:38.510
outselling apples and oranges combined by about 1925.

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:43.030
And that number continues in those proportions today.

00:16:45.104 --> 00:16:48.854
And again, I said bananas are the cheapest fruit in the supermarket.

00:16:48.864 --> 00:16:49.544
So

00:16:49.984 --> 00:16:53.524
you guys probably call what you see here where it says this hand

00:16:54.144 --> 00:16:55.254
a bunch of bananas.

00:16:55.264 --> 00:16:59.814
But in fact, the bunch is the hundred or so bananas that grow in one clump on a

00:16:59.824 --> 00:17:04.614
tree. A hand is what you buy at Trader Joe's, and each individual banana is called

00:17:04.624 --> 00:17:08.134
a finger. These bananas were 10 cents for a hand.

00:17:08.144 --> 00:17:12.534
That was about half the price of the same number of apples and poundage.

00:17:12.544 --> 00:17:16.534
And then the next picture, you see a fellow falling and slipping on a banana peel.

00:17:16.544 --> 00:17:21.374
Well, that's an old slapstick joke, but the banana companies were so successful that they

00:17:22.032 --> 00:17:23.382
Actually, this became a crisis.

00:17:23.392 --> 00:17:26.902
People would throw banana peels on streets and they would slip on them.

00:17:26.912 --> 00:17:28.262
In fact, in some cities like St.

00:17:28.272 --> 00:17:32.102
Louis, laws were made against throwing banana peels on the ground.

00:17:32.112 --> 00:17:35.332
In New York City, where some of you guys are,

00:17:36.032 --> 00:17:37.902
the banana problem became so

00:17:38.656 --> 00:17:42.886
Ubiquitous that the city actually founded a sanitation corps.

00:17:42.896 --> 00:17:46.726
So the garbage trucks that you see picking up your trash, if you're lucky on the streets

00:17:46.736 --> 00:17:49.646
of Brooklyn and Queens, where like where I grew up.

00:17:50.544 --> 00:17:55.364
are a direct result of banana litter in the early 1900s.

00:17:55.424 --> 00:18:00.214
So once those sanitation services came into being, the slipping on the banana

00:18:00.224 --> 00:18:04.790
sort of ended, but it remains a mildly funny joke.

00:18:06.160 --> 00:18:09.830
Okay, let's move to the, you know, the way the bananas are in the rest of the world.

00:18:09.840 --> 00:18:11.460
These are pictures I took

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:15.414
about 10 years ago in the Democratic Republic of Congo.

00:18:16.512 --> 00:18:20.822
And in Congo, especially in eastern Congo,

00:18:20.832 --> 00:18:24.582
bananas can account for as much as 80% of people's

00:18:24.592 --> 00:18:28.982
calories. And so, when bananas are there, times are good.

00:18:28.992 --> 00:18:33.302
And when bananas are not there because of disease or market

00:18:33.312 --> 00:18:35.222
factors, times are not good.

00:18:35.232 --> 00:18:39.622
And so, when we see genocide and fighting in places like Rwanda,

00:18:39.632 --> 00:18:44.022
Burundi, Eastern Congo, Kivu Province, where these pictures were

00:18:44.032 --> 00:18:45.262
taken.

00:18:47.488 --> 00:18:52.278
You begin to understand that hungry people are desperate people, and desperate

00:18:52.288 --> 00:18:56.598
people. Both turn to desperate measures and are subject to desperate measures.

00:18:56.608 --> 00:19:00.198
And so, right now, in this picture, you see ubiquitous bananas.

00:19:00.208 --> 00:19:04.518
But, you know, maybe five years later, these places were emptied out of

00:19:04.528 --> 00:19:08.998
bananas, and many people starved, and many people took up arms, and many people died.

00:19:09.008 --> 00:19:13.478
And so, it's not just a cool fruit that we can tell these funny stories

00:19:13.488 --> 00:19:16.998
about. It's a really, really important crop.

00:19:17.008 --> 00:19:21.078
I would argue, and I'm a banana guy, so the wheat guys will disagree, the rice guys will

00:19:21.088 --> 00:19:23.278
disagree, but I would say the banana is.

00:19:23.616 --> 00:19:26.006
probably the most critical food crop in the world.

00:19:26.016 --> 00:19:30.726
Maybe not the most important, but there's no other crop that if you lose it, so

00:19:30.736 --> 00:19:32.726
many disastrous things happen.

00:19:32.736 --> 00:19:36.446
And we are losing bananas because of banana disease.

00:19:38.176 --> 00:19:42.116
I talked a little bit about, you know, we showed the atheist tests.

00:19:42.336 --> 00:19:44.726
These are actually wild bananas.

00:19:44.736 --> 00:19:46.836
They have nothing to do with people.

00:19:46.896 --> 00:19:48.326
They are very small.

00:19:48.336 --> 00:19:52.006
They are almost inedible because as you can see in the picture, the close-up, there are

00:19:52.016 --> 00:19:55.286
seeds in them, big seeds that are very hard and will break your teeth.

00:19:55.296 --> 00:19:58.886
The little bit of fruit in them does taste okay, not super sweet.

00:19:58.896 --> 00:20:01.446
But the point is bananas evolved from these.

00:20:01.456 --> 00:20:05.526
When a seedless banana was found, it was grown through grafting.

00:20:05.536 --> 00:20:10.326
And that is basically how the banana in a really simplified form sort of became went from

00:20:10.336 --> 00:20:14.966
this wild fruit, which you can still find in Asia and in parts of Africa, to

00:20:14.976 --> 00:20:18.870
this giant multi-billion dollar commercial industry.

00:20:20.944 --> 00:20:25.574
Bananas are important because a singa tree, like in this picture, can feed an entire

00:20:25.584 --> 00:20:30.484
village. A single banana mini plantation or garden in the

00:20:31.264 --> 00:20:33.894
other picture can feed an entire region.

00:20:33.904 --> 00:20:37.974
Bananas produce between 100 and 300 fruits per year all at

00:20:37.984 --> 00:20:39.454
once. And so

00:20:40.096 --> 00:20:44.166
If you're in a village, you want to have a bunch of trees because the big tree might

00:20:44.176 --> 00:20:48.086
produce in April and the tree next one might produce in March.

00:20:48.096 --> 00:20:49.686
And this encourages commerce.

00:20:49.696 --> 00:20:53.606
You know, if you have a tree in your yard that produced in April and your neighbor has

00:20:53.616 --> 00:20:57.126
one that produced in June, you're going to trade the bananas with them because you can't

00:20:57.136 --> 00:20:58.326
eat them all.

00:20:58.336 --> 00:21:03.006
So literally, you know, these bananas are providing sort of shade.

00:21:03.888 --> 00:21:07.268
Nutrition and life to these communities.

00:21:07.408 --> 00:21:11.878
And this picture was taken off the Congo River in a

00:21:11.888 --> 00:21:16.518
small village called Yangambi, only accessible

00:21:16.528 --> 00:21:18.068
by water.

00:21:18.208 --> 00:21:20.000
And there's some weird bananas.

00:21:20.120 --> 00:21:23.230
Red bananas, really long bananas.

00:21:23.240 --> 00:21:26.990
Someone's probably asking what is the difference between bananas and plantains.

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:30.190
There isn't really a major biological difference.

00:21:30.200 --> 00:21:33.070
It's that plantains develop less sugar.

00:21:33.080 --> 00:21:37.790
They're starchier, so they're more effective as a sort of potato substitute, but both

00:21:37.800 --> 00:21:39.158
are equally important.

00:21:40.160 --> 00:21:44.790
And then banana disease, when we see some sick banana plants here, they're

00:21:44.800 --> 00:21:46.422
drooping, they're dry.

00:21:47.136 --> 00:21:51.606
Because bananas are a monoculture commercially and because they're close to a

00:21:51.616 --> 00:21:56.166
monoculture, there are about a thousand banana varieties, but really only 10 to 15

00:21:56.176 --> 00:21:58.086
major families.

00:21:58.096 --> 00:22:01.366
What makes one banana sick can make almost all bananas sick.

00:22:01.376 --> 00:22:04.246
And so when bananas get sick, it's devastating.

00:22:04.256 --> 00:22:08.726
These diseases spread across commercial plantations, across regions, across villages.

00:22:08.736 --> 00:22:10.086
People lose their jobs.

00:22:10.096 --> 00:22:11.366
People lose their livelihoods.

00:22:11.376 --> 00:22:12.406
People starve.

00:22:13.728 --> 00:22:15.198
Um and uh

00:22:15.808 --> 00:22:17.878
Here's a healthy banana crop.

00:22:17.888 --> 00:22:19.318
Well, the one on the left is not healthy.

00:22:19.328 --> 00:22:20.918
You can see the black spots on it.

00:22:20.928 --> 00:22:22.918
It's got a freckle disease.

00:22:22.928 --> 00:22:25.588
This gentleman on the right is a banana grower.

00:22:25.808 --> 00:22:29.158
I believe this was taken in Uganda by me.

00:22:29.168 --> 00:22:31.078
And he's got some banana shoots.

00:22:31.088 --> 00:22:34.998
So when the bananas are interesting because they can grow two ways by the seeds for the

00:22:35.008 --> 00:22:39.078
wild bananas, but also for the cultivated bananas by pulling these shoots out of the

00:22:39.088 --> 00:22:41.558
ground and plugging them into another part of ground.

00:22:41.568 --> 00:22:46.278
So these are often called mother daughter plants or what he's holding are called

00:22:46.288 --> 00:22:47.558
banana suckers.

00:22:47.568 --> 00:22:52.468
You can take a banana sucker and throw it in the back of your home in a village

00:22:52.688 --> 00:22:55.278
and it'll keep for five years and you can grow it again.

00:22:55.552 --> 00:23:00.452
And back in ancient times, when people traveled, say, from the

00:23:00.752 --> 00:23:05.462
central Indian Ocean all the way to Tanzania, they probably carried these bananas with

00:23:05.472 --> 00:23:08.182
them, knowing that they could plant them when they reached shore.

00:23:08.192 --> 00:23:11.782
And so the banana literally, as I said earlier, follows us around the world because of

00:23:11.792 --> 00:23:13.142
its ease of planting.

00:23:14.416 --> 00:23:16.596
Again, here's some different varieties.

00:23:17.056 --> 00:23:20.566
And here's on the other side is this young man with a bicycle.

