01:36:57.712 --> 01:37:01.172
The Mutika Lujagira group is from

01:37:02.192 --> 01:37:04.292
the East African highlands.

01:37:04.352 --> 01:37:07.342
They're really interesting in the sense that they are.

01:37:08.336 --> 01:37:12.966
The staple food of Uganda in particular, but in many regions of East

01:37:12.976 --> 01:37:15.126
Africa, and they're eaten, steamed, and cooked.

01:37:15.136 --> 01:37:18.996
And what a lot of people don't realize about bananas is that probably

01:37:19.856 --> 01:37:23.166
even with cabendish being the largest.

01:37:23.600 --> 01:37:28.230
You know, or the most widely grown banana variety, and bananas themselves being

01:37:28.240 --> 01:37:33.220
one of the most widely grown crops in the world and probably the most widely grown

01:37:33.440 --> 01:37:36.340
fresh produce item traded in the world.

01:37:36.480 --> 01:37:41.110
About 50% of all bananas grown in the world are cooking varieties, and

01:37:41.120 --> 01:37:42.870
they're eaten as a starchy staple.

01:37:42.880 --> 01:37:47.270
So, this is just an example of what that looks like: the culture behind it in

01:37:47.280 --> 01:37:52.022
Uganda. The fruit's peeled, it's wrapped in banana leaves,

01:37:52.032 --> 01:37:56.662
they're bundled up and tied with banana stem fibers, and then they're

01:37:56.672 --> 01:37:59.702
steamed in a pot and it comes out something like mashed potatoes.

01:37:59.712 --> 01:38:04.102
And this is really a major, major food in East

01:38:04.112 --> 01:38:08.132
Africa. And there's a lot of different varieties of these types of

01:38:08.272 --> 01:38:10.862
bananas with many different traits.

01:38:12.096 --> 01:38:16.806
This is a photo I took in Uganda that I thought was just very emblematic

01:38:16.816 --> 01:38:18.406
of the whole scene there.

01:38:18.416 --> 01:38:22.806
Proud to be Ugandan on the truck with some guys hanging out with their piles of

01:38:22.816 --> 01:38:27.142
motoke bananas, just selling them on the side of the road.

01:38:28.736 --> 01:38:33.556
There's also varieties in East Africa that are traditionally fermented.

01:38:33.616 --> 01:38:38.166
They taste a little bit bitter and astringent if you were to eat them on their own, but

01:38:38.176 --> 01:38:42.966
they've been reserved and domesticated and cultivated for thousands of years

01:38:42.976 --> 01:38:44.916
specifically for fermenting

01:38:45.456 --> 01:38:46.966
into an alcohol.

01:38:46.976 --> 01:38:50.000
So you see this kind of canoe-looking thing, the fruit.

01:38:50.040 --> 01:38:52.270
Is peeled and mashed in there.

01:38:52.280 --> 01:38:56.670
You throw in some banana leaves and other things, stomp around, and eventually some juice

01:38:56.680 --> 01:39:01.310
comes out. You might throw in a little bit of roasted sorghum to kind of help

01:39:01.320 --> 01:39:02.830
the ferment go.

01:39:02.840 --> 01:39:07.310
But this is a widespread traditional use in East

01:39:07.320 --> 01:39:08.630
Africa as well.

01:39:09.312 --> 01:39:12.902
Okay, plantins, we're going to talk about them for a minute and really what they are.

01:39:12.912 --> 01:39:17.142
There's a really big misconception in the sort of layman's banana world that there's

01:39:17.152 --> 01:39:19.782
bananas and plantains as if they're two different things.

01:39:19.792 --> 01:39:22.262
But it's kind of like saying dogs and chihuahuas.

01:39:22.272 --> 01:39:23.542
They're not equal.

01:39:23.552 --> 01:39:26.932
They're different categories, you know, they're different ranks.

01:39:26.992 --> 01:39:31.382
So all plantains are bananas, but it's just a really specific genetic group.

01:39:31.392 --> 01:39:34.422
And it's not even synonymous with cooking bananas.

01:39:34.432 --> 01:39:38.982
There's a lot of bananas like the ones from Uganda that I was just showing that are

01:39:38.992 --> 01:39:41.172
cooked that are not plantains.

01:39:41.232 --> 01:39:44.982
There's three kind of main morphological groups of plantains though.

01:39:44.992 --> 01:39:49.222
We got French types which have a lot of fruit and fully developed

01:39:49.232 --> 01:39:53.942
flowers. We have false horn types with pretty big fruit but less of them and

01:39:53.952 --> 01:39:56.342
the flowers kind of fade away.

01:39:56.352 --> 01:40:00.662
And then we have true horn type plantins which only make one cluster of fruit but they

01:40:00.672 --> 01:40:05.382
get really big sometimes up to two feet long and about three

01:40:05.392 --> 01:40:06.886
pounds per fruit.

01:40:07.904 --> 01:40:11.094
In Hawaii and the Pacific, we have the Maulipopoulu.

01:40:11.104 --> 01:40:14.564
These also follow a similar sort of morphological

01:40:15.584 --> 01:40:17.414
track with the plantains.

01:40:17.424 --> 01:40:21.574
They make typically very large bunches of very large fruit, and this would have been a

01:40:21.584 --> 01:40:25.158
traditional staple in many parts of the Pacific.

01:40:25.920 --> 01:40:30.260
You can see here on the right, this was some whole cooked fruits.

01:40:30.400 --> 01:40:31.910
This is in raw tonga.

01:40:31.920 --> 01:40:36.070
Some varieties, such as the tanga, akamo, or kuamoa we have here in

01:40:36.080 --> 01:40:40.630
Hawaii, make these big fat round fruits that are you know maybe about

01:40:40.640 --> 01:40:43.140
four inches across sometimes.

01:40:43.280 --> 01:40:45.910
And you can eat them raw, but they're also traditionally cooked.

01:40:45.920 --> 01:40:49.270
And if you get it just when they're a little bit starchy and a little bit sweet, I think

01:40:49.280 --> 01:40:51.470
it's one of the best foods in the world.

01:40:52.672 --> 01:40:56.582
Iholena is another group that we have in the Pacific and Hawaii.

01:40:56.592 --> 01:41:00.742
They typically start off actually kind of yellow, which is unusual, although there are

01:41:00.752 --> 01:41:02.582
some red varieties too.

01:41:02.592 --> 01:41:05.422
And they ripen more yellow, but they also have

01:41:06.384 --> 01:41:10.534
Sorry, yellow flesh inside, which gives them a really interesting

01:41:10.544 --> 01:41:13.894
appearance. They're also very quick to fruit.

01:41:13.904 --> 01:41:18.534
So we have a dwarf variety here in Hawaii that I grow, and I've been able

01:41:18.544 --> 01:41:21.574
to harvest them in as little as seven months after planting.

01:41:21.584 --> 01:41:25.734
A typical banana might be in the 10 to 14 month

01:41:25.744 --> 01:41:30.454
range. So this is just an example of them growing on my farm from July to

01:41:30.464 --> 01:41:34.790
December, tiny little plants all the way up to getting ready to harvest.

01:41:35.888 --> 01:41:40.198
Dilk. This is what a variety that you might find as apple bananas or

01:41:40.208 --> 01:41:42.918
monzano, rarely in grocery stores.

01:41:42.928 --> 01:41:46.868
It's pretty tasty, one of the most commonly grown bananas in the world.

01:41:47.328 --> 01:41:49.478
Quite good if you get a chance to try it.

01:41:50.096 --> 01:41:51.606
Home, also known as Prata.

01:41:51.616 --> 01:41:54.916
This is what Thanos had mentioned, is his favorite banana.

01:41:55.056 --> 01:41:56.726
You could go to Brazil and get it.

01:41:56.736 --> 01:42:00.006
It's also the most common variety grown in Hawaii.

01:42:00.016 --> 01:42:03.446
If you ever come out here and you had apple bananas, this is what we call apple in

01:42:03.456 --> 01:42:04.294
Hawaii.

01:42:04.992 --> 01:42:06.822
Mysore is another one.

01:42:06.832 --> 01:42:10.502
I think that this actually kind of tastes a bit like raspberries.

01:42:10.512 --> 01:42:14.742
If you ever wanted to identify these plants out in the field, they always have a reddish

01:42:14.752 --> 01:42:18.758
underside of the leaf, and that's one way that you can tell that you have a myre.

01:42:19.264 --> 01:42:23.334
Blugo, this is also called guru or largo, many different

01:42:23.344 --> 01:42:28.054
names. Sometimes they're a bit waxy and they kind of have a bluish hue to

01:42:28.064 --> 01:42:32.334
the fruit of sabah, which is

01:42:32.816 --> 01:42:37.286
Typically looks like a classic type banana, but there is a mutation called praying

01:42:37.296 --> 01:42:40.756
hands in which the fruit is fused together.

01:42:40.816 --> 01:42:45.636
And if you cut through a whole hand of it, it kind of looks like a honeycomb.

01:42:45.936 --> 01:42:48.070
So just another fun weirdo.

01:42:48.512 --> 01:42:53.142
Pisang a wok or the namwas, they come in tall forms and short forms and waxy

01:42:53.152 --> 01:42:55.222
forms and waxless forms.

01:42:55.232 --> 01:42:59.382
If you ever had bananas in Thailand or Cambodia or

01:42:59.392 --> 01:43:02.310
Vietnam, most likely was this one.

01:43:03.328 --> 01:43:07.878
Um, we talk a lot about the fruit, and when we think about bananas, diversity, we like to

01:43:07.888 --> 01:43:12.598
think about the fruit often, but I kind of view bananas in a totally different way, and

01:43:12.608 --> 01:43:14.678
I kind of look at every single part of the plant.

01:43:14.688 --> 01:43:19.078
And this is just to show these are just the male flower buds on different varieties,

01:43:19.088 --> 01:43:23.318
which, if you ever are up close and personal with the plants, most people notice them as

01:43:23.328 --> 01:43:26.198
like, what's that big purple thing hanging off?

01:43:26.208 --> 01:43:29.878
But actually, they can be quite diverse in their form and

01:43:29.888 --> 01:43:31.348
coloration.

01:43:31.408 --> 01:43:34.388
And this isn't really very important at all for

01:43:35.568 --> 01:43:39.518
growing bananas, but I think it's really interesting.

01:43:40.848 --> 01:43:44.198
Useful information to have if you're trying to identify different varieties, you're

01:43:44.208 --> 01:43:48.678
getting to know them. These male buds have a lot of information and clues to their

01:43:48.688 --> 01:43:50.000
genetic background.

01:43:50.000 --> 01:43:52.486
So it's something that I personally study quite a lot.

01:43:53.504 --> 01:43:55.814
All right, that was all just one section of the genus.

01:43:55.824 --> 01:43:58.694
So we're going to talk about calimusa, which is a whole different section.

01:43:58.704 --> 01:44:03.014
So now we're back to wild species, but these are sort of like a different group of wild

01:44:03.024 --> 01:44:04.454
species of bananas.

01:44:04.464 --> 01:44:07.014
Kalimusa means the beautiful bananas.

01:44:07.024 --> 01:44:10.164
This is just an example of a few of the wild species.

01:44:10.224 --> 01:44:14.294
Many of them aren't cultivated at all, but the few that are are often grown for

01:44:14.304 --> 01:44:18.564
ornamental purposes, either just landscape plants or cut flowers.

01:44:18.784 --> 01:44:23.284
It's just an example of a few different species of the calimusas.

01:44:23.344 --> 01:44:26.644
In particular, the Vietnamese calimusa, I think, are

01:44:27.184 --> 01:44:30.974
very beautiful and they have a lot of different forms.

01:44:32.016 --> 01:44:36.356
And it's a group that I've been working a lot with that I think is

01:44:36.976 --> 01:44:40.036
ripe for more development.

01:44:40.736 --> 01:44:44.996
If you ever in northern Vietnam, you might see these around hotels

01:44:45.616 --> 01:44:47.046
and restaurants.

01:44:47.056 --> 01:44:49.926
These are wild banana musa splendida.

01:44:49.936 --> 01:44:52.582
This is in Thapa in Vietnam.

01:44:54.208 --> 01:44:58.918
This is another species from this group, native more to Papua New Guinea.

01:44:58.928 --> 01:45:03.078
What's interesting with them that's a bit unusual from other types of

01:45:03.088 --> 01:45:05.878
bananas is that they have upright bunches.

01:45:05.888 --> 01:45:09.268
So instead of hanging down, they go straight up in the air.

01:45:09.328 --> 01:45:12.918
They have a deep orange flesh, high in carotenoids.

01:45:12.928 --> 01:45:16.548
And the stack is either like purple or red.

01:45:16.608 --> 01:45:20.918
And these are the most likely wild ancestors to a group of

01:45:20.928 --> 01:45:25.478
bananas that we call feti bananas, which are completely unrelated to everything else we

01:45:25.488 --> 01:45:28.868
were talking about before. They just come from this species.

01:45:29.168 --> 01:45:33.798
Feti are traditionally grown in the Pacific, but they're very

01:45:33.808 --> 01:45:35.158
poorly understood.

01:45:35.168 --> 01:45:39.158
There's many varieties that we don't quite understand how they all relate to each other,

01:45:39.168 --> 01:45:42.534
but this is an example of a few of them that I've documented.

01:45:42.992 --> 01:45:43.672
Um

01:45:44.528 --> 01:45:48.868
There's many different forms, but they all

01:45:49.168 --> 01:45:51.638
are really, really difficult to grow plants.

01:45:51.648 --> 01:45:55.558
And so, actually, traditionally in the Pacific, they're not even really cultivated, so

01:45:55.568 --> 01:45:59.878
much is just planted out in remote areas of the forest, and then they would go

01:45:59.888 --> 01:46:04.388
harvest them. So, this is in Rarotonga and Takubaina Valley.

01:46:04.448 --> 01:46:09.238
There's thousands and thousands of them all over the forest, but someone had to

01:46:09.248 --> 01:46:10.998
go put them there at some point.

01:46:11.008 --> 01:46:15.318
But the people today they know that they say their ancestors planted them, but nobody

01:46:15.328 --> 01:46:19.838
today is planting them, but they'll still go and harvest them from the forest.

01:46:20.880 --> 01:46:25.350
This is a particularly interesting garden in Rowatonga from a

01:46:25.360 --> 01:46:29.990
woman, Celine Dyer, who maintained a fati collection in

01:46:30.000 --> 01:46:33.430
Rowatonga. They call them Utu, so I'm calling them Utu here.

01:46:33.440 --> 01:46:37.380
But this is to show how their skin and flesh look.

01:46:37.600 --> 01:46:40.420
Super, super high in carotenoids.

01:46:40.560 --> 01:46:44.390
And when you eat them, you can't really absorb all that provitamin A

01:46:44.400 --> 01:46:45.510
carotenoids.

01:46:45.520 --> 01:46:49.380
And so it gets extremed in your urine really quickly.

01:46:49.520 --> 01:46:53.188
And so there's some kind of like You know, jokes and

01:46:53.808 --> 01:46:54.918
references to it.

01:46:54.928 --> 01:46:59.398
And Papua New Guinea in the local pigeon, they'll often call this yellow pispis

01:46:59.408 --> 01:47:03.828
banana because, like, after you eat it, yeah, you have really yellow pispis, like

01:47:03.968 --> 01:47:05.718
extraordinarily yellow.

01:47:05.728 --> 01:47:07.838
So, kind of fun, different uh

01:47:08.656 --> 01:47:12.726
Variety to have. And like I mentioned too, the stap is purple or red.

01:47:12.736 --> 01:47:16.886
And so when you cut through it, it kind of looks like it's bleeding, which is used also

01:47:16.896 --> 01:47:21.236
as a traditional dye in many parts of the Pacific to dye different

01:47:21.696 --> 01:47:23.910
textiles that were produced.

01:47:24.272 --> 01:47:26.452
This is the fruit of it cooked.

01:47:26.992 --> 01:47:28.182
Super, super good.

01:47:28.192 --> 01:47:32.182
You can eat them raw. I really prefer them cooked and cooked with coconut milk.

01:47:32.192 --> 01:47:33.742
Insanely delicious.

01:47:34.704 --> 01:47:39.254
Musaschizocarpa, this is a variety from mainland Papua New Guinea and its name

01:47:39.264 --> 01:47:41.414
means splitting fruit.

01:47:41.424 --> 01:47:45.014
So when the fruit is ripe, it splits open.

01:47:45.024 --> 01:47:49.734
This is an ancestral parent to some varieties in Papua New Guinea, but

01:47:49.744 --> 01:47:54.214
we've actually found through more modern genetic tools that there are

01:47:54.224 --> 01:47:58.374
traces of this species in almost every single edible variety, even in

01:47:58.384 --> 01:48:01.382
Cavendish, which wasn't known until a couple years ago.

01:48:02.992 --> 01:48:06.662
These are some of the hybrids with Schizocarpa and Acada.

01:48:06.672 --> 01:48:09.622
They tend to have a really sour, weird flavor to them.

01:48:09.632 --> 01:48:14.262
I really don't particularly like that, but they are a very

01:48:14.272 --> 01:48:17.862
unique group of bananas that is also poorly understood.

01:48:18.960 --> 01:48:23.670
Between the different sections, which in Botany 101, if there's any nerds

01:48:23.680 --> 01:48:27.430
out there thinking about chromosome counts, these shouldn't be able to cross, but nature

01:48:27.440 --> 01:48:31.750
always finds a way, and sometimes they do cross, especially in Papua New Guinea.

01:48:31.760 --> 01:48:36.310
We find some varieties like this that defy all classification, and I just call them

01:48:36.320 --> 01:48:38.374
intersectional outliers.

01:48:39.504 --> 01:48:43.574
Here's some more that are tetraploids that are very similar to well-known triploid

01:48:43.584 --> 01:48:44.884
varieties, but

01:48:45.584 --> 01:48:47.654
much bigger, more robust plants.

01:48:47.664 --> 01:48:50.000
And what's interesting genetically, again, is.

01:48:50.000 --> 01:48:53.550
That these, if you were to try to produce these in a breeding program, you probably

01:48:53.560 --> 01:48:57.580
couldn't do it, but they exist out there in

01:48:58.200 --> 01:48:59.870
villages out in the world.

01:48:59.880 --> 01:49:04.590
And so it's really inspiring for me from a banana diversity standpoint and from a

01:49:04.600 --> 01:49:09.390
breeding standpoint that the possibilities of what we can do if we start combining

01:49:09.400 --> 01:49:12.070
different genetics is really limitless.

01:49:12.080 --> 01:49:15.510
And on that note, I actually am producing my own varieties of bananas.

01:49:15.520 --> 01:49:17.630
So you may have heard that bananas are.

01:49:18.176 --> 01:49:19.526
Um, difficult to breed.

01:49:19.536 --> 01:49:22.646
Some people say they're impossible to breed, which is definitely not true.

01:49:22.656 --> 01:49:27.156
They're difficult in the sense that seeds are a necessary

01:49:27.296 --> 01:49:32.006
problem. So, what makes bananas good to eat is that they typically don't have seeds in

01:49:32.016 --> 01:49:36.246
them, but in order to create new varieties, you need that sexual

01:49:36.256 --> 01:49:39.206
recombination and the production of seed.

01:49:39.216 --> 01:49:43.446
But then, hopefully, those seeds grow into plants that also don't want to make seed, so

01:49:43.456 --> 01:49:48.166
it gets a little bit weird, but it doesn't stop me or anyone from still going

01:49:48.176 --> 01:49:51.926
for it. So, I'm breeding all kinds of new varieties.

01:49:51.936 --> 01:49:55.316
Some of them are more ornamental, some of them are more edible.

01:49:55.536 --> 01:49:59.446
This is just an example of some wacky stuff that's popped out of my breeding

01:49:59.456 --> 01:50:04.166
efforts. And I do have some also edible varieties that I'm just

01:50:04.176 --> 01:50:07.156
starting to kind of get characterized.

01:50:07.216 --> 01:50:11.126
These have broad disease resistance, but also really good flavor.

01:50:11.136 --> 01:50:12.996
So, these are three of my

01:50:13.696 --> 01:50:15.078
prototype.

01:50:15.776 --> 01:50:19.366
Sort of varieties that I've been working on for a number of years.

01:50:19.376 --> 01:50:20.726
Two of them have names.

01:50:20.736 --> 01:50:24.566
One of them is probably not going to release it as anything, but it's just going to

01:50:24.576 --> 01:50:27.716
continue to be a parent to breed other varieties.

01:50:27.776 --> 01:50:30.406
The lookout for Lime Sherbirds coming in hot.

01:50:31.728 --> 01:50:35.958
And with that, I wanted to keep it short and sweet, but I'm open to any questions.

01:50:35.968 --> 01:50:40.278
Feel free to follow us on Instagram, Hawaii Banana Source, or check out our website.

01:50:40.288 --> 01:50:40.750
And I

01:50:40.750 --> 01:50:45.478
am happy to take any questions that anybody has about anything I said or

01:50:45.488 --> 01:50:47.366
didn't say about bananas.

01:50:53.488 --> 01:50:54.678
Thank you, Gabe.

01:50:54.688 --> 01:50:56.948
Okay, so we will do the chat.

01:50:57.088 --> 01:50:59.428
Thanos, did you have a question for

01:51:00.448 --> 01:51:03.428
Gabe or do you just wanted to applause?

01:51:03.808 --> 01:51:07.622
What is the flavor profile of your banana that you're creating?

01:51:08.832 --> 01:51:13.462
Well, I right now I probably have about 5,000 seedlings in the nursery and

01:51:13.472 --> 01:51:14.982
each is a different unique plant.

01:51:14.992 --> 01:51:17.942
So I don't know until it's finalized.

01:51:17.952 --> 01:51:22.262
But for lime sherbert, which is kind of my favorite one that I'm getting excited

01:51:22.272 --> 01:51:26.662
about, it has a really tart flavor that's a bit

01:51:26.672 --> 01:51:31.028
pineapple-like, but aromatic on top of that

01:51:31.088 --> 01:51:33.758
slightly orange flesh um

01:51:34.544 --> 01:51:37.894
But also a really thick, nice texture.

01:51:37.904 --> 01:51:40.854
It's very like satisfying to take a bite of.

01:51:40.864 --> 01:51:43.204
It's not like mushy or soft.

01:51:43.264 --> 01:51:46.774
And I call it lime because it ripens a little bit green.

01:51:46.784 --> 01:51:50.534
And so when it's ready, it kind of has like a green fruit, but it's really tart.

01:51:50.544 --> 01:51:54.566
So I thought lime and sherbert because of the orange flush.

01:51:55.456 --> 01:51:56.756
Amazing.

01:51:57.456 --> 01:51:58.196
Okay.

01:51:58.736 --> 01:52:00.406
Does anyone else have any?

01:52:00.416 --> 01:52:02.116
Has anyone ever changed

01:52:03.136 --> 01:52:04.356
like the

01:52:05.216 --> 01:52:06.806
how do you change the interior color?

01:52:06.816 --> 01:52:10.966
Can you do that through like a directed evolution to make it blue or other

01:52:10.976 --> 01:52:12.356
colors?

